{"trial_id": "chat_decode-r1", "request_sha256": "f87634689dc9e0f2518ea6273add10a58cb01207fe6bf0008e93cb6caab6d43d", "finish_reasons": ["length"], "http_status": 200, "client_ttft_seconds": 0.011847904417663813, "client_elapsed_seconds": 11.73096178099513, "output_text": "A library organizes and lends books through a carefully coordinated system that begins before a book reaches a shelf and continues long after it leaves the building. The process is designed to make materials easy to find, easy to borrow, easy to track, and easy to return, while also protecting the collection from loss, damage, and confusion. At the center of this system is cataloging, which is the method by which a library describes each item so that it can be identified, located, and managed. When a new book arrives, catalogers examine its title, author, publisher, edition, subject matter, and physical details. They then assign a call number, often using a classification system such as Dewey Decimal Classification or Library of Congress Classification, which places the book in a logical subject area. This call number acts like an address, telling staff and patrons where the book belongs on the shelf. Cataloging also creates a record in the library’s computer system, which may include the book’s title, author, publication date, subject headings, and a unique barcode. That barcode becomes the book’s identity in circulation, allowing staff to check it out, check it in, track its history, and connect it to the catalog record. Without cataloging, a library would be a large collection of objects with no reliable way to find them or manage them.\n\nOnce cataloging is complete, the book is prepared for circulation. This often includes adding protective covers, spine labels, and sometimes security devices. The book is then shelved according to its call number, so that related works are grouped together and can be found by browsing as well as by searching the catalog. In a modern library, the catalog is not just a list of titles; it is a dynamic record of availability. It shows whether a book is on the shelf, checked out", "tool_call_deltas": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 88, "completion_tokens": 512, "total_tokens": 600, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}}, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 88, "prompt_ms": 1077.8, "prompt_per_token_ms": 12.248, "prompt_per_second": 81.6, "predicted_n": 512, "predicted_ms": 10632.3, "predicted_per_token_ms": 21.479, "predicted_per_second": 46.6, "draft_n": 444, "draft_n_accepted": 273}, "server_load_seconds": 25.215}
{"trial_id": "chat_decode-r2", "request_sha256": "f87634689dc9e0f2518ea6273add10a58cb01207fe6bf0008e93cb6caab6d43d", "finish_reasons": ["length"], "http_status": 200, "client_ttft_seconds": 0.012646303977817297, "client_elapsed_seconds": 11.50015948805958, "output_text": "A library organizes and lends books through a coordinated system of cataloging, holding, circulation, returns, and inventory, each step supporting the next so that materials can be found, tracked, and made available to readers. Cataloging is the beginning of this process because it creates the record that allows a book to be identified and located. When a new book arrives, catalogers examine its title, author, publisher, edition, physical details, and subject matter. They then assign a classification number and call number, often using a system such as Dewey Decimal Classification or Library of Congress Classification, so that books with similar subjects can be shelved together. They also create a bibliographic record containing the book’s title, author, publication information, and other descriptive details. This record is entered into the library’s catalog, which may be a card catalog, a computer database, or an online public access catalog. The catalog record is essential because it tells patrons what the library has, where it is located, and how to request it. Without cataloging, a book would be physically present but practically invisible to the public.\n\nOnce a book is cataloged, it becomes part of the library’s holdings, which are the collection of materials the library owns or manages. Holdings records show how many copies exist, where each copy is located, and whether a copy is available, checked out, damaged, lost, or reserved. In a large library, holdings may be distributed among several branches, departments, or special collections, so the holdings record helps staff and patrons understand which copy is in which location. Some libraries also maintain electronic holdings, such as e-books, databases, or digital journals, which require different cataloging and access methods but still need to be tracked as part of the collection. Holdings information is closely connected to circulation because it determines", "tool_call_deltas": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 88, "completion_tokens": 512, "total_tokens": 600, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}}, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 88, "prompt_ms": 1483.0, "prompt_per_token_ms": 16.852, "prompt_per_second": 59.3, "predicted_n": 512, "predicted_ms": 9993.8, "predicted_per_token_ms": 20.149, "predicted_per_second": 49.6, "draft_n": 410, "draft_n_accepted": 276}, "server_load_seconds": 93.438}
{"trial_id": "chat_decode-r3", "request_sha256": "f87634689dc9e0f2518ea6273add10a58cb01207fe6bf0008e93cb6caab6d43d", "finish_reasons": ["length"], "http_status": 200, "client_ttft_seconds": 0.01136758690699935, "client_elapsed_seconds": 11.279253222048283, "output_text": "A library organizes and lends books through a carefully coordinated system that begins before a book reaches a shelf and continues long after it leaves the library. The process is designed to make materials easy to find, easy to borrow, easy to track, and easy to return, while also protecting the collection from loss, damage, and confusion. Although modern libraries use computers, scanners, and online catalogs, the underlying principles remain the same: identify each item, record its location, manage its movement, and keep accurate records of what the library owns and where it is.\n\nCataloging is the first major step in organizing a library’s collection. When a new book arrives, it is not simply placed on a shelf. It must be described, classified, and assigned a call number so that it can be found by both staff and patrons. Cataloging begins with bibliographic description, which includes the title, author, publisher, year of publication, edition, and other details that identify the work. The cataloger also determines the subject of the book and assigns a classification number, such as Dewey Decimal or Library of Congress Classification, depending on the library’s system. This classification places the book within a broad subject area, while the call number gives it a precise shelf location. For example, a book about the history of the American Civil War might be classified under history and then given a specific number that distinguishes it from other books on the same subject. Cataloging also includes the creation of records in the library’s catalog, which may be a local system or a shared network. These records allow patrons to search by title, author, subject, keyword, or publication date. In many libraries, cataloging is also linked to metadata standards and controlled vocabularies, which help ensure that similar works are grouped together and that searches return useful results.", "tool_call_deltas": [], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 88, "completion_tokens": 512, "total_tokens": 600, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}}, "timings": {"cache_n": 0, "prompt_n": 88, "prompt_ms": 1065.0, "prompt_per_token_ms": 12.102, "prompt_per_second": 82.6, "predicted_n": 512, "predicted_ms": 10191.8, "predicted_per_token_ms": 20.589, "predicted_per_second": 48.6, "draft_n": 451, "draft_n_accepted": 273}, "server_load_seconds": 94.155}
